1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
It arrives from outer space, blows in with the wind, rides in with the waves, and rises from the molten core of the Earth, just in time to save a planet in crisis.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Earth, air, fire, water, and imagination.

3
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Limitless resources whose time has come.

4
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, renewable energy on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The action never stops at the FedEx Regional Sort Facility.

6
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:57,000
Located at Oakland International Airport in California, it's the

7
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:01,000
shipping giant's hub for the Pacific region.

8
00:01:01,001 --> 00:01:05,000
1,700 workers and a state-of-the-art conveyor system

9
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:10,000
sort about 85 million packages per year.

10
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:13,000
There's more than a quarter of a million packages that transit

11
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:15,640
that transit this facility in a given day.

12
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:19,700
And we, in a month, are using approximately 450,000

13
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:24,140
to 500,000 kilowatt hours of power.

14
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:26,540
To meet these electricity demands,

15
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,060
FedEx decided to build its own power plant.

16
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,860
It's silent, has no moving parts, and it's located conveniently

17
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,260
on the roof.

18
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,160
Taking advantage of abundant sunshine and 81,000 square

19
00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:44,660
of unused roof space, FedEx hired Sharp Solar

20
00:01:44,661 --> 00:01:49,700
to design this photovoltaic electricity system in 2005.

21
00:01:49,701 --> 00:01:52,200
Today, it supplies 80% of the facility's

22
00:01:52,201 --> 00:01:54,700
peak electricity needs.

23
00:01:54,701 --> 00:01:58,520
We're looking at a system here that's 904 kilowatts,

24
00:01:58,521 --> 00:02:00,120
a very large system.

25
00:02:00,121 --> 00:02:03,120
It might be the largest system, rooftop system,

26
00:02:03,121 --> 00:02:06,020
on a commercial building in the state of California.

27
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:10,160
And it's composed of about 5,700 solar modules.

28
00:02:10,161 --> 00:02:16,160
And that large array uses about 300,000 silicon cells.

29
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:21,640
When sunlight strikes each of the silicon cells,

30
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:26,080
it excites electrons, which are knocked free to form an electric current.

31
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:32,560
Metal contact points carry the current away and complete the circuit.

32
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,820
Typically, 40 solar cells make up a module.

33
00:02:38,821 --> 00:02:42,820
Several modules are combined into an array.

34
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:46,820
The power off of these arrays are ganged up,

35
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:49,820
put together, and sent down through an inverter.

36
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:54,820
That inverter changes DC power to AC power.

37
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:56,820
And that's it.

38
00:02:56,821 --> 00:03:00,820
In a split second, sunlight is converted into electricity,

39
00:03:00,821 --> 00:03:03,820
enough to power the beehive of activity below.

40
00:03:03,821 --> 00:03:06,820
It can power a light bulb on a desk,

41
00:03:06,821 --> 00:03:09,660
or it could power the whole sort system.

42
00:03:09,661 --> 00:03:12,160
One of the large benefits of solar power

43
00:03:12,161 --> 00:03:15,820
for commercial applications is that the system typically

44
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:18,660
produces its maximum amount of power

45
00:03:18,661 --> 00:03:20,660
when utility prices are the highest,

46
00:03:20,661 --> 00:03:22,660
which is the middle of the day.

47
00:03:22,661 --> 00:03:25,580
When this system is putting out more power

48
00:03:25,581 --> 00:03:27,580
than the building is using,

49
00:03:27,581 --> 00:03:29,580
the power goes back into the grid

50
00:03:29,581 --> 00:03:32,820
and actually turns the meter backwards.

51
00:03:32,821 --> 00:03:36,820
And the system is pretty much maintenance-free.

52
00:03:36,821 --> 00:03:40,220
So we're very pleased with that, and it's just another way that

53
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:44,220
FedEx can lower our environmental footprint and

54
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:48,460
still give great service to our customers.

55
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:50,260
What kind of environmental benefit

56
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,560
can one rooftop bring?

57
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:55,280
Over its 25-year guaranteed life,

58
00:03:55,380 --> 00:04:00,280
this one will offset 10,800 tons of carbon dioxide.

59
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,180
That's the equivalent of removing 2,100 cars from the road.

60
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:09,720
In an economy burdened by the costs of increasingly scarce

61
00:04:09,721 --> 00:04:14,320
oil and natural gas, and a planet already feeling

62
00:04:14,321 --> 00:04:17,960
the impact of fossil fuel-driven climate change,

63
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:20,460
the technological revolution of our time

64
00:04:20,461 --> 00:04:22,960
is renewable energy.

65
00:04:22,961 --> 00:04:25,480
And say welcome to the 21st century.

66
00:04:25,481 --> 00:04:27,080
Things are going to be different.

67
00:04:27,081 --> 00:04:28,980
Energy, sustainable energy, is something

68
00:04:28,981 --> 00:04:32,020
that our kids care about and the mainstream public cares

69
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:33,020
about.

70
00:04:33,021 --> 00:04:35,880
And renewables has to be part of the solution.

71
00:04:35,881 --> 00:04:43,120
sunlight, wind, water, geothermal heat, and plant

72
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:48,820
biomass are all essentially limitless resources.

73
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:51,220
And they're all proven technologies that work right

74
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:54,640
here, right now.

75
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:59,040
The sun provides the Earth with unfathomable amounts of energy.

76
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:02,380
In one hour, the Earth receives more energy from the sun than

77
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:08,380
human race uses in a year. With current technology,

78
00:05:08,381 --> 00:05:13,380
photovoltaic panels covering 10,000 square miles, or about 10%

79
00:05:13,381 --> 00:05:16,380
of the land area of Nevada, could provide all of the

80
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:19,380
electricity used by the United States.

81
00:05:19,381 --> 00:05:23,380
Although photovoltaics, converting the sun's light to

82
00:05:23,381 --> 00:05:27,380
electricity, was not discovered until the 20th century, solar

83
00:05:27,381 --> 00:05:32,380
thermal, harnessing the sun's heat, is an ancient practice.

84
00:05:32,381 --> 00:05:35,980
Perhaps the earliest use of solar thermal technology was the

85
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:40,620
south-facing home. Ancient cultures, including the

86
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:44,220
Anasazi Indians of the American Southwest, designed their

87
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:48,620
buildings to face south, to maximize heat from the sun, which

88
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:52,940
stays low in the southern sky during winter.

89
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,440
In the summer, when the sun is high in the sky, the cliff face

90
00:05:56,540 --> 00:06:00,840
that extended over the building provided cooling shade.

91
00:06:00,841 --> 00:06:04,840
It's still a common design today.

92
00:06:04,841 --> 00:06:09,840
The Greeks built entire cities, laying out the streets

93
00:06:09,841 --> 00:06:13,840
so every house could take advantage of sunlight during

94
00:06:13,841 --> 00:06:16,840
winter but avoid it during summer.

95
00:06:16,841 --> 00:06:19,840
Now, the Romans went one step further,

96
00:06:19,841 --> 00:06:24,840
and they discovered that clear glass will trap solar heat.

97
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:27,840
So they covered the windows with glass.

98
00:06:27,841 --> 00:06:31,640
They became what the Romans called heliocomini,

99
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:34,480
which mean solar furnaces.

100
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:37,480
Two millennia later, the 19th century equivalent

101
00:06:37,481 --> 00:06:42,180
of this technology was born, the rooftop solar water heater,

102
00:06:42,181 --> 00:06:46,200
which traps the sun's rays in a glass collector

103
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:51,200
and rapidly heats up water inside insulated glass pipes.

104
00:06:51,201 --> 00:06:54,700
At the turn of the 20th century, 30% of the homes

105
00:06:54,701 --> 00:06:57,100
of the homes in Pasadena, California,

106
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:00,400
used solar water heaters.

107
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:04,000
The same concept is used today to cheaply provide hot water

108
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:08,240
to millions, even in cold, cloudy climates.

109
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:11,360
For almost every homeowner, heating water

110
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:14,660
is the number one use of natural gas electricity.

111
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:19,600
And using the sun to heat is much cheaper.

112
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,660
Solar thermal energy can also cheaply

113
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:24,660
produce electricity.

114
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:27,700
Giant solar power plants like this one in California's

115
00:07:27,701 --> 00:07:31,700
Mojave Desert don't use photovoltaic panels to convert

116
00:07:31,701 --> 00:07:34,700
light into electricity.

117
00:07:34,701 --> 00:07:38,700
Instead, mirrors focus solar rays onto a thin absorber tube

118
00:07:38,701 --> 00:07:42,700
that runs along the mirror's focal point.

119
00:07:42,701 --> 00:07:45,700
Inside the tube is synthetic oil, which is blasted with

120
00:07:45,701 --> 00:07:49,700
intense heat. Like about 1,000 degrees

121
00:07:49,701 --> 00:07:52,700
centigrade. One word of advice, never put your

122
00:07:52,701 --> 00:07:56,940
put your hand at the focus of a concentrating mirror,

123
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:00,140
because it will just make an immediate hole

124
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:03,040
through your flush. The superheated oil

125
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:06,940
boils water into steam, which turns turbines.

126
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:11,060
The oil then recirculates through the closed loop system.

127
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:13,960
In hot, dry areas, solar thermal plants

128
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:18,100
generate serious megawatts at a price lower than typical grid

129
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:21,800
power. In fact, solar thermal plants

130
00:08:21,801 --> 00:08:26,140
occupying that same 10% section of Nevada could also supply all

131
00:08:26,141 --> 00:08:30,840
of the United States' electrical needs.

132
00:08:30,841 --> 00:08:34,640
Meanwhile, photovoltaic technology is surging ahead

133
00:08:34,641 --> 00:08:39,660
into its second century. It's come a long way since 1904,

134
00:08:39,661 --> 00:08:42,760
when Albert Einstein first proved that light was composed of

135
00:08:42,761 --> 00:08:46,200
tiny packets of energy known as photons, which could be

136
00:08:46,201 --> 00:08:51,100
converted to electricity. Photovoltaic cells got their

137
00:08:51,101 --> 00:08:57,600
big break in the 1950s, thanks to the space race.

138
00:08:57,601 --> 00:09:00,200
The first two Sputniks that went up,

139
00:09:00,201 --> 00:09:02,940
they had all this, like, multimillion dollars

140
00:09:02,941 --> 00:09:05,140
worth of transistors.

141
00:09:05,141 --> 00:09:07,440
That went dead in about a week and a half,

142
00:09:07,441 --> 00:09:10,660
because they were running them on flashlight batteries.

143
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:14,060
The first artificial Earth satellite in the world

144
00:09:14,061 --> 00:09:15,560
has now been created.

145
00:09:15,561 --> 00:09:18,060
The US space program's solar-powered satellites,

146
00:09:18,061 --> 00:09:21,560
on the other hand, proved an instant success.

147
00:09:21,561 --> 00:09:24,900
Photovoltaic panels still power nearly all satellites

148
00:09:24,901 --> 00:09:27,740
and space probes within the solar system.

149
00:09:27,741 --> 00:09:30,700
In this sense, the 21st century lifestyle,

150
00:09:30,701 --> 00:09:33,880
from global positioning to global communication,

151
00:09:33,881 --> 00:09:37,820
is completely dependent on solar energy.

152
00:09:37,821 --> 00:09:41,020
The US Coast Guard became one of the first Earth-based users

153
00:09:41,021 --> 00:09:44,580
of photovoltaics, powering its remote buoys and weather

154
00:09:44,581 --> 00:09:48,580
with solar panels, wired to batteries.

155
00:09:48,581 --> 00:09:52,580
In the 1970s and 80s, solar panels performed heroically

156
00:09:52,581 --> 00:09:55,580
during one of the worst droughts to hit Africa.

157
00:09:55,581 --> 00:09:59,580
Solar power was used to pump water from 40 feet below the

158
00:09:59,581 --> 00:10:02,580
parched ground, saving many lives.

159
00:10:02,581 --> 00:10:04,580
In fact, probably one of the most valuable uses of

160
00:10:04,581 --> 00:10:09,580
photovoltaics today is to provide clean drinking water for

161
00:10:09,581 --> 00:10:11,580
the developing world.

162
00:10:11,581 --> 00:10:14,580
For those already connected to the power grid,

163
00:10:14,581 --> 00:10:17,580
photovoltaics are becoming more and more viable.

164
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:21,880
A home, say a two-bedroom suburban American home,

165
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:27,820
gets enough energy on its roof to power itself once or twice

166
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:28,820
over.

167
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:31,640
And it doesn't actually need to be as sunny as you think

168
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:33,140
for solar to work.

169
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:35,240
The most successful solar program in the world

170
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:37,840
is actually in Germany, and they have less sun in Germany

171
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:39,840
than they do in Seattle.

172
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:42,080
For the time being, photovoltaic panels

173
00:10:42,180 --> 00:10:43,940
are still expensive.

174
00:10:43,941 --> 00:10:47,940
making solar electricity two to five times the cost of typical

175
00:10:47,941 --> 00:10:51,940
grid power. But grid-connected homeowners

176
00:10:51,941 --> 00:10:54,940
can sell excess peak hour electricity back to the power

177
00:10:54,941 --> 00:10:59,940
company at a premium. The systems do pay for themselves.

178
00:10:59,941 --> 00:11:03,940
The payback might be as short as four or five years, and in

179
00:11:03,941 --> 00:11:07,940
general, for residential systems, we're looking at 10 to 12 years.

180
00:11:07,941 --> 00:11:10,940
That doesn't take into account some of the other benefits, like,

181
00:11:10,941 --> 00:11:13,480
like, for example, the protection from rising utility rates,

182
00:11:13,481 --> 00:11:15,280
or also increased property value.

183
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:22,180
Up until now, the primary cost of solar cells

184
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:24,380
has been the ultra-pure silicon needed

185
00:11:24,381 --> 00:11:26,880
for the photovoltaic reaction.

186
00:11:26,881 --> 00:11:30,400
But more than one company wants to change that.

187
00:11:30,401 --> 00:11:33,100
The photovoltaics industry is based primarily

188
00:11:33,101 --> 00:11:36,940
on wafers of silicon to make high-efficiency solar cells.

189
00:11:36,941 --> 00:11:39,900
But those wafers themselves are very expensive.

190
00:11:39,901 --> 00:11:43,700
Nanosolar uses thin films of photoactive material instead

191
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:47,100
of those expensive wafers.

192
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:51,340
Rather than silicon, nanosolar in Palo Alto, California,

193
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:55,540
uses a combination of semiconducting elements.

194
00:11:55,640 --> 00:12:00,260
So you have copper, indium, gallium, sulfo, selenide.

195
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:02,460
Because that's obviously a mouthful, we get more work done

196
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:05,700
if we just say CIGS, C-I-G-S-S.

197
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:08,960
The beauty of the material absorbs sunlight very readily,

198
00:12:08,961 --> 00:12:12,300
so you can make a very thin layer.

199
00:12:12,301 --> 00:12:14,760
So thin that the absorbing material

200
00:12:14,761 --> 00:12:19,500
is assembled on the nanoscale, then literally printed

201
00:12:19,501 --> 00:12:24,200
onto a foil substrate as a photovoltaic ink.

202
00:12:24,201 --> 00:12:28,420
On this experimental machine, the foil moving very slowly,

203
00:12:28,421 --> 00:12:32,420
but this style of machine can be made to run at tens

204
00:12:32,421 --> 00:12:35,520
or even hundreds of meters per minute.

205
00:12:35,521 --> 00:12:38,960
And because of this, these relatively inexpensive machines

206
00:12:38,961 --> 00:12:42,960
can be used to make an enormous quantity of low-cost

207
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:46,960
photovoltaic cells.

208
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:49,640
Thin-film solar panels, as well as their silicon

209
00:12:49,740 --> 00:12:53,380
counterparts, can be built right into a building surface,

210
00:12:53,480 --> 00:12:58,720
such as a roof, window glass, or in the near future, paint.

211
00:12:58,820 --> 00:13:01,480
So-called building-integrated photovoltaics

212
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:05,980
will drive down the cost of solar electricity even further.

213
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:08,960
Because you're not buying panels and buildings

214
00:13:08,961 --> 00:13:10,460
you're buying it all at once.

215
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,660
And my lab has been looking at forecasts

216
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:18,300
of getting 20%, 25% of total energy from solar

217
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:20,300
by making this ubiquitous material

218
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:22,300
that you just get everywhere.

219
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:24,300
The sun's heat also gives rise

220
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:29,080
to the invisible, powerful force known as the wind.

221
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:31,680
And in the future of energy, wind

222
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:33,680
is a force to be reckoned with.

223
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:42,460
Solar water heaters are mandatory for all residences

224
00:13:42,461 --> 00:13:44,460
in Israel.

225
00:13:44,461 --> 00:13:47,460
Other governments are considering similar requirements.

226
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:01,460
Tucked among the rolling hills of central New York

227
00:14:01,461 --> 00:14:06,460
is the Fenner Wind Project, owned by NL North America.

228
00:14:06,461 --> 00:14:09,100
Though not the world's biggest wind power project,

229
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:12,560
it represents the new thinking in wind energy.

230
00:14:12,660 --> 00:14:16,500
Bigger, smarter, and fewer wind turbines,

231
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:21,400
eliminating the need for dozens of smaller turbines.

232
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:24,520
FINR is composed of 20 wind turbines built and maintained

233
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:27,520
by GE Energy.

234
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:33,620
Each stands 215 feet tall, has a rotor 218 feet in diameter,

235
00:14:33,621 --> 00:14:38,920
diameter, a bit longer than the wingspan of a 747, and produces

236
00:14:38,921 --> 00:14:44,360
1.5 megawatts of electricity. When you look at the average

237
00:14:44,361 --> 00:14:47,860
New York household, this wind power project can actually power

238
00:14:47,861 --> 00:14:51,680
about 14,000 homes over the course of a year.

239
00:14:51,681 --> 00:14:54,980
Converting wind to electricity begins as the wind flows over

240
00:14:54,981 --> 00:15:00,320
the rotor's airfoil-shaped blades. The combination of lift and

241
00:15:00,321 --> 00:15:03,820
and drag causes the blades to rotate.

242
00:15:03,821 --> 00:15:06,520
There's a tremendous amount of kinetic energy in the wind.

243
00:15:06,521 --> 00:15:10,020
And similar to sailing or lifting an aircraft off the ground,

244
00:15:10,021 --> 00:15:12,360
the wind blade captures that kinetic energy

245
00:15:12,361 --> 00:15:13,780
and provides lift.

246
00:15:13,781 --> 00:15:18,220
And that lift, like a propeller, is converted into torque.

247
00:15:18,221 --> 00:15:20,120
That tremendous torque is put to work

248
00:15:20,121 --> 00:15:24,960
inside the turbine's power plant, 200 feet above the ground.

249
00:15:24,961 --> 00:15:28,120
The blades collect the energy from the wind

250
00:15:28,121 --> 00:15:32,560
and convert that energy from wind energy to mechanical energy,

251
00:15:32,561 --> 00:15:36,040
transmits it through the main shaft into the gearbox,

252
00:15:36,041 --> 00:15:39,160
which the gearbox steps it up from about one revolution

253
00:15:39,161 --> 00:15:43,220
to 70 revolutions and puts it on the output shaft of the gearbox

254
00:15:43,221 --> 00:15:46,920
