temperature. And if you get that balance

508
00:31:32,560 --> 00:31:35,000
right, the engine runs great. It even can run better

509
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:38,000
than on diesel.

510
00:31:38,001 --> 00:31:43,840
The best fuel is actually used vegetable oil,

511
00:31:43,980 --> 00:31:47,280
since the frying process has cooked out any embedded water.

512
00:31:48,580 --> 00:31:50,700
So if you work a deal with the restaurant,

513
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:54,000
pick it up for free, you're saving them a fortune,

514
00:31:54,001 --> 00:31:55,360
and you're saving yourself a fortune.

515
00:31:56,820 --> 00:31:58,720
That's because the restaurant avoids

516
00:31:58,721 --> 00:32:02,380
its usual oil disposal fee, and the driver gets free fuel.

517
00:32:02,381 --> 00:32:08,900
Of course, new vegetable oil works just about as well.

518
00:32:10,460 --> 00:32:15,420
Cotton seed seems to run the best, but soybean is the easiest to find.

519
00:32:15,520 --> 00:32:17,080
It runs great, and it's the cheapest.

520
00:32:17,540 --> 00:32:20,280
So we're really advocates of soybean oil.

521
00:32:20,780 --> 00:32:22,200
It can bail out farmers.

522
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:24,304
It keeps the money in the U.S.

523
00:32:24,305 --> 00:32:27,560
economy, and there's already an infrastructure in place.

524
00:32:27,561 --> 00:32:30,800
While the idea of running a car on cooking oil may hold a

525
00:32:30,801 --> 00:32:35,360
certain comic appeal, vegetable oil fuel is part of the solution

526
00:32:35,361 --> 00:32:40,800
to a deadly serious problem. Nearly 20% of the world's global

527
00:32:40,801 --> 00:32:45,920
warming greenhouse gases come directly from automobiles.

528
00:32:45,921 --> 00:32:51,920
In addition to being renewable, biofuels from vegetable oil to

529
00:32:51,921 --> 00:32:57,620
to bio-diesel to ethanol have at least two environmental benefits

530
00:32:57,621 --> 00:32:59,620
over gasoline.

531
00:32:59,621 --> 00:33:02,960
They release fewer and less toxic emissions.

532
00:33:02,961 --> 00:33:05,460
And more importantly, their carbon emissions

533
00:33:05,461 --> 00:33:08,300
can potentially be offset by the carbon taken out

534
00:33:08,301 --> 00:33:10,800
of the atmosphere when the biofuel was a plant.

535
00:33:13,680 --> 00:33:16,920
Bio-diesel, which is made from a plant's fatty oils,

536
00:33:16,921 --> 00:33:20,920
and ethanol, an alcohol fuel distilled from a plant's sugars,

537
00:33:20,921 --> 00:33:22,720
are the hot biofuels of the moment.

538
00:33:26,220 --> 00:33:30,720
But they're also straight out of early automotive history.

539
00:33:30,721 --> 00:33:34,460
Rudolph Diesel built his engine to run on peanut oil.

540
00:33:34,461 --> 00:33:38,880
Henry Ford designed the Model T to run on ethanol.

541
00:33:38,881 --> 00:33:42,180
Since then, the onset of petroleum and gasoline

542
00:33:42,181 --> 00:33:45,680
came into play, and it was a lot cheaper to do than ethanol.

543
00:33:45,681 --> 00:33:48,720
We weren't nearly as efficient producing it.

544
00:33:48,721 --> 00:33:50,880
Each new discovery of cheap domestic oil

545
00:33:50,980 --> 00:33:54,560
pushed these early biofuels to the margins.

546
00:33:54,660 --> 00:33:57,520
And now we're doing almost a full circle turn back

547
00:33:57,620 --> 00:34:01,460
to go to what we were utilizing in the first place.

548
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:04,360
In the US, the most talked about ethanol feedstock

549
00:34:04,460 --> 00:34:06,580
is corn grain.

550
00:34:06,680 --> 00:34:09,380
But corn grain ethanol yields only slightly more energy

551
00:34:09,480 --> 00:34:14,680
than the amount of fossil fuels needed to harvest and distill it.

552
00:34:14,780 --> 00:34:18,680
For every unit of petroleum that you put in,

553
00:34:18,720 --> 00:34:23,060
production process, you get 1.3 units of fuel ethanol energy

554
00:34:23,061 --> 00:34:24,060
out.

555
00:34:24,061 --> 00:34:28,460
So it's slightly positive, but it's not as good as it could be.

556
00:34:28,461 --> 00:34:32,560
By contrast, the sugarcane ethanol used in Brazil yields

557
00:34:32,561 --> 00:34:36,480
eight units of energy for every unit of fossil energy put in.

558
00:34:36,481 --> 00:34:38,880
Not only is the feedstock bursting with fermentable

559
00:34:38,881 --> 00:34:42,820
sugars, but the rest of the plant is burned cleanly to power the

560
00:34:42,821 --> 00:34:46,720
fermenting and distilling.

561
00:34:46,721 --> 00:34:50,420
The result is a major reduction in carbon emissions

562
00:34:50,520 --> 00:34:52,720
and dirt cheap ethanol for Brazilian drivers.

563
00:34:56,660 --> 00:34:58,760
At the National Renewable Energy Laboratory

564
00:34:58,860 --> 00:35:01,560
in Golden, Colorado, researchers aim

565
00:35:01,660 --> 00:35:03,300
to hit similar levels of efficiency

566
00:35:03,400 --> 00:35:06,300
by extracting sugars from the entire plant, which

567
00:35:06,400 --> 00:35:10,880
includes the so-called cellulosic biomass.

568
00:35:10,980 --> 00:35:12,420
Corn farmers will be happy to know

569
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:16,720
that one of the more promising crops is corn stover.

570
00:35:16,721 --> 00:35:21,460
It's the stalks, the husks, the corn cobs, the leaves,

571
00:35:21,560 --> 00:35:24,960
every part of the corn plant, aside from the grain,

572
00:35:25,060 --> 00:35:27,460
that's not harvested when you harvest the grain.

573
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:30,460
So oftentimes, it's left laying out in the field.

574
00:35:30,560 --> 00:35:36,380
There's not a lot of current uses for it.

575
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:39,380
The hard part is converting the tough cellulose fibers

576
00:35:39,480 --> 00:35:43,280
into sugars using bioengineered enzymes.

577
00:35:43,380 --> 00:35:46,720
But the payoff could be tremendous.

578
00:35:46,721 --> 00:35:50,320
With biomass, we're talking ratios on the order of 10 to 1.

579
00:35:50,321 --> 00:35:53,280
So for every unit of fossil energy or petroleum

580
00:35:53,281 --> 00:35:55,020
that you put into the process, you

581
00:35:55,021 --> 00:35:59,680
get 10 units of fuel energy out the back end.

582
00:35:59,681 --> 00:36:02,360
The tall prairie grass, known as switchgrass,

583
00:36:02,361 --> 00:36:06,080
may prove an even more powerful biofuel crop.

584
00:36:06,081 --> 00:36:09,680
Not only can you get more tonnage of biomass out

585
00:36:09,681 --> 00:36:12,360
of an acre of land with this type of material

586
00:36:12,361 --> 00:36:14,720
than you can, say, with corn stover,

587
00:36:14,721 --> 00:36:17,180
But there are a lot of other benefits as well.

588
00:36:17,181 --> 00:36:20,320
It doesn't take nearly the amount of water or fertilizer

589
00:36:20,321 --> 00:36:21,120
to make it grow.

590
00:36:21,121 --> 00:36:22,620
It's very fast growing.

591
00:36:22,621 --> 00:36:26,460
You can harvest it once, maybe even twice in a year,

592
00:36:26,461 --> 00:36:29,840
and has a wide geographic range across which it can grow,

593
00:36:29,841 --> 00:36:34,580
North Dakota all the way down to Texas, to the southeast.

594
00:36:34,581 --> 00:36:37,240
Biofuels aren't the only way for renewable energy

595
00:36:37,241 --> 00:36:39,280
to clean up the automobile.

596
00:36:39,281 --> 00:36:41,940
There's also the power grid.

597
00:36:41,941 --> 00:36:44,980
This is a plug-in hybrid.

598
00:36:44,981 --> 00:36:47,840
Like other hybrids, it runs on a gasoline engine

599
00:36:47,841 --> 00:36:49,880
and an electric motor, powered in part

600
00:36:49,881 --> 00:36:52,540
by the momentum of braking.

601
00:36:52,541 --> 00:36:55,800
But Felix Kramer and the California Cars Initiative

602
00:36:55,801 --> 00:36:58,440
have taken these already fuel-efficient cars,

603
00:36:58,441 --> 00:37:03,100
in this case a Toyota Prius, a step further.

604
00:37:03,101 --> 00:37:06,060
They've beefed up the battery pack

605
00:37:06,061 --> 00:37:08,740
and converted it to run on electricity taken straight

606
00:37:08,741 --> 00:37:13,480
from any 120-volt outlet. Plugging this car in is really

607
00:37:13,481 --> 00:37:16,640
easy. I timed it once. It's about nine seconds.

608
00:37:16,641 --> 00:37:20,080
All you really do is you take that, plug it into here, 120

609
00:37:20,081 --> 00:37:24,720
volts in your garage, nothing special, and you're done.

610
00:37:24,721 --> 00:37:28,660
The gasoline is used only as a range extension fuel.

611
00:37:28,661 --> 00:37:32,700
Under 35 miles per hour, not a drop is used.

612
00:37:32,701 --> 00:37:36,060
Above that speed, the gas and electric motors combine for an

613
00:37:36,061 --> 00:37:41,260
average of 100 miles per gallon. And the range of this car is

614
00:37:41,261 --> 00:37:44,300
unlimited because it's also a gasoline car.

615
00:37:44,301 --> 00:37:47,500
This is a car that you get to plug in, and you can plug it in

616
00:37:47,501 --> 00:37:50,140
whenever it's convenient. You're not worried, uh-oh, am I

617
00:37:50,141 --> 00:37:53,980
going to run out of power? Cars that get 100 miles per

618
00:37:53,981 --> 00:37:57,500
gallon and more will be necessary if the world's farmlands are to

619
00:37:57,501 --> 00:38:02,140
grow fuel as well as food. With current MPG standards,

620
00:38:02,141 --> 00:38:05,500
there's simply not enough acreage to supplant the world's oil habit

621
00:38:05,501 --> 00:38:11,000
without endangering its food supplies.

622
00:38:11,100 --> 00:38:14,840
In 2006, the brilliantly simple plug-in prototypes

623
00:38:14,940 --> 00:38:17,140
greatly impressed senators and representatives

624
00:38:17,240 --> 00:38:19,940
from both sides of the aisle.

625
00:38:20,040 --> 00:38:21,840
And the Congresswoman introduced me,

626
00:38:21,940 --> 00:38:23,960
and I stood up, and I said, I'm here.

627
00:38:24,060 --> 00:38:26,660
And actually, I brought my infrastructure with me.

628
00:38:26,760 --> 00:38:28,560
And that got a big laugh, because this is the only

629
00:38:28,660 --> 00:38:31,100
infrastructure that a car needs.

630
00:38:31,200 --> 00:38:33,800
Of course, electricity today is generated largely

631
00:38:33,801 --> 00:38:37,500
fossil fuels. But plug-in proponents envision

632
00:38:37,501 --> 00:38:42,400
an increasingly green power grid to charge the car's battery,

633
00:38:42,401 --> 00:38:46,240
and cellulosic biofuels to fill the tank.

634
00:38:46,241 --> 00:38:49,040
There are some corporations that are talking about installing

635
00:38:49,041 --> 00:38:52,060
solar carports where they have photovoltaic cells on the roofs

636
00:38:52,061 --> 00:38:54,860
of their parking garages, and people with plug-in hybrids or

637
00:38:54,861 --> 00:38:58,300
pure electric vehicles can plug in during the day.

638
00:38:58,301 --> 00:39:01,800
While biofuels and plug-in hybrids surge ahead,

639
00:39:01,801 --> 00:39:06,240
A new wave of renewable technology is coming in with the tide.

640
00:39:09,540 --> 00:39:12,140
The estimated ethanol yield for switchgrass

641
00:39:12,141 --> 00:39:15,640
is 1,150 gallons per acre.

642
00:39:15,641 --> 00:39:20,200
Currently, sugarcane yields about 662 gallons per acre

643
00:39:20,201 --> 00:39:23,040
and corn grain 354 gallons.

644
00:39:31,800 --> 00:39:38,800
Despite the fact that the oceans cover 70% of the planet's surface,

645
00:39:38,801 --> 00:39:43,800
we've only begun to tap them directly as an energy source.

646
00:39:43,801 --> 00:39:47,800
But these marine powerhouses may one day unlock the door to clean,

647
00:39:47,801 --> 00:39:52,800
limitless energy. Hydroelectric power was the great

648
00:39:52,801 --> 00:39:56,800
renewable energy source of the 20th century, and it will continue to

649
00:39:56,801 --> 00:40:00,800
and it will continue to provide gigawatts of power.

650
00:40:00,900 --> 00:40:04,000
But with most of the world's rivers already dammed,

651
00:40:04,100 --> 00:40:09,440
the future of hydropower will come from the sea.

652
00:40:09,540 --> 00:40:12,840
Of the various technologies to capture the ocean's energy,

653
00:40:12,940 --> 00:40:16,260
a tidal barrage is the closest in design

654
00:40:16,360 --> 00:40:21,260
to traditional hydropower.

655
00:40:21,360 --> 00:40:24,160
The barrage is essentially a dam, collecting water

656
00:40:24,161 --> 00:40:27,200
from high tide until there's sufficient potential energy

657
00:40:27,300 --> 00:40:31,000
for power generation, then forcing the water through

658
00:40:31,100 --> 00:40:34,800
turbines when released during low tide.

659
00:40:34,900 --> 00:40:37,900
The world's largest and oldest tidal barrage at Laurence,

660
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:42,920
France, began producing power in 1966.

661
00:40:43,020 --> 00:40:46,620
Today, it still powers 240,000 homes.

662
00:40:49,520 --> 00:40:53,420
A more recent technology known as tidal stream power uses

663
00:40:53,421 --> 00:40:56,720
uses propeller-like turbines to capture the kinetic energy

664
00:40:56,721 --> 00:40:59,160
of underwater currents.

665
00:40:59,161 --> 00:41:01,560
And that's one that we could do in all manner of places,

666
00:41:01,561 --> 00:41:02,820
because the tidal flow, of course,

667
00:41:02,821 --> 00:41:04,620
takes place everywhere on Earth.

668
00:41:04,621 --> 00:41:07,820
And harvesting those could be done quite well,

669
00:41:07,821 --> 00:41:09,760
and doesn't even have to be at the surface.

670
00:41:09,761 --> 00:41:11,520
The issue, though, is that the marine environment's

671
00:41:11,521 --> 00:41:13,060
pretty tough to deal with.

672
00:41:13,061 --> 00:41:16,260
There's corrosion, parts being banged back and forth.

673
00:41:16,261 --> 00:41:19,260
Often, the cost of managing those in terms of repairs

674
00:41:19,261 --> 00:41:20,780
can be quite large.

675
00:41:20,781 --> 00:41:23,580
But we're seeing new work on that.

676
00:41:23,680 --> 00:41:26,680
Much of the work is taking place in the British Isles,

677
00:41:26,780 --> 00:41:29,180
where companies such as marine current turbines

678
00:41:29,280 --> 00:41:32,620
are thinking big, sinking massive pilings

679
00:41:32,720 --> 00:41:38,700
into the ocean floor, and letting the turbines do their work.

680
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:41,860
And the amount of energy you get out of a turbine

681
00:41:41,960 --> 00:41:45,280
is proportional directly to the density of the fluid that

682
00:41:45,380 --> 00:41:46,580
flows back and forth.

683
00:41:46,680 --> 00:41:50,380
And so a water turbine has several hundred times

684
00:41:50,381 --> 00:41:53,480
power of an air turbine.

685
00:41:53,580 --> 00:41:56,820
Wave power is an even newer technology,

686
00:41:56,920 --> 00:41:59,420
yet it may hold the most promise of all,

687
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:03,320
since wave power can be captured anywhere on the ocean.

688
00:42:03,420 --> 00:42:07,300
In this design, a 450-foot chain of pipes connected

689
00:42:07,400 --> 00:42:12,000
by hinged joints sits semi-submerged on the ocean.

690
00:42:12,100 --> 00:42:15,500
Each motion of the waves is resisted by hydraulic rams

691
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:17,740
located in the hinges.

692
00:42:17,741 --> 00:42:19,780
This action pumps high-pressure oil

693
00:42:19,880 --> 00:42:24,480
through hydraulic motors, which drive electrical generators.

694
00:42:24,580 --> 00:42:26,180
Electrical transmission lines also

695
00:42:26,280 --> 00:42:31,180
keep the floating power plant tethered to the ocean floor.

696
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:34,180
A Scottish company, Ocean Power Delivery,

697
00:42:34,280 --> 00:42:38,200
has installed a successful 2.5 megawatt wave farm

698
00:42:38,300 --> 00:42:41,000
off the coast of Portugal.

699
00:42:41,100 --> 00:42:45,040
A wave farm occupying less than a half a square mile of ocean

700
00:42:45,041 --> 00:42:51,040
megawatts of power, or enough for 20,000 British homes.

701
00:42:51,041 --> 00:42:56,040
472 square miles of ocean could power each of the United Kingdom's

702
00:42:56,041 --> 00:42:59,040
24 and a half million households.

703
00:42:59,041 --> 00:43:05,040
Each region of the Earth has its own renewable sources, from the

704
00:43:05,041 --> 00:43:09,040
vast wind potential of North Dakota to the solar mother load

705
00:43:09,041 --> 00:43:12,040
that waits in the Sahara.

706
00:43:12,041 --> 00:43:17,040
When you put them all together, one thing becomes clear.

707
00:43:17,140 --> 00:43:19,580
Renewable energy offers a global solution

708
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:21,980
to global problems.

709
00:43:22,080 --> 00:43:23,580
We honestly have the potential now

710
00:43:23,680 --> 00:43:25,480
to think about flipping things from being

711
00:43:25,580 --> 00:43:29,020
a fossil fuel-driven economy with little bits of renewables

712
00:43:29,120 --> 00:43:31,720
to being a renewable energy-driven economy

713
00:43:31,820 --> 00:43:34,000
with fossil fuels filling in the background.

714
00:43:34,100 --> 00:43:37,440
And that's a world that really no one thought possible only

715
00:43:37,540 --> 00:43:40,440
a decade or two ago.

716
00:43:40,441 --> 00:43:44,740
The technology works, but a renewable future also

717
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:50,380
requires political will and a new way of thinking,

718
00:43:50,480 --> 00:43:56,920
in which a farm harvests wind, a harvest yields fuel,

719
00:43:57,020 --> 00:43:59,820
and a rooftop becomes a power plant.

